      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Rendering</td>
        <td>Canvas 2D</td>
        <td
          >Direct pixel control, no DOM overhead, smooth pan/zoom</td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Deployment</td>
        <td>GitHub Pages</td>
        <td>Free static hosting, one-click deploy via GitHub Actions</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Module Map</h2>
  <pre><code>src/
  pages/
    index.astro            Page shell: file picker, canvas, controls, event wiring
    docs/                  This documentation

  lib/
    graph-types.ts         Type definitions (wire format + resolved model)
    graph-model.ts         Deserialization and model construction
    grid-layout.ts         Layer assignment, ordering, routing, coordinate calc
    grid-types.ts          Grid data structures (LayoutGrid, PlacedNode, RoutingArea)
    spatial-index.ts       Grid-hash spatial index for viewport culling and hit detection
    renderer.ts            Scene management, render loop, viewport culling, LOD, selection
    viewport.ts            Pan, zoom, keyboard, and touch interaction
    canvas-constants.ts    Grid unit (12 px/GU) and all sizing constants
    canvas-primitives.ts   Low-level canvas utilities (rounded rects, paths, text)
    draw-node.ts           Node body, ports, toggle button
    draw-edge.ts           Edge paths, arrowheads, crossing markers
    draw-subgraph.ts       Subgraph frame, boundary ports
    color-utils.ts         Hex/RGB/HSL conversion, color manipulation, edge palette</code></pre>

  <h2>Data Pipeline</h2>
  <p>Data goes through four stages:</p>
  <pre><code>JSON file  ──&gt;  buildGraphModel()  ──&gt;  computeAllLayouts()  ──&gt;  computeAllRouting()  ──&gt;  render()
  │                  │                       │                        │                     │
  wire format     GraphModel              ScopeLayout map          ScopeRouting map      Canvas pixels
  (flat arrays)   (nodeMap, scopeMap)     (grid-unit positions)    (pixel waypoints)     (draw calls)</code></pre>

  <p>
    Each stage is a pure function of the previous stage's output, so it's
    easy to follow and test.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Canvas, Not SVG or DOM?</h2>
  <p>
    Computation graphs can have thousands of nodes and edges. SVG creates a DOM
    element for each one, which gets slow when panning and zooming. Canvas draws
    everything in one paint pass with no layout reflow. With viewport culling,
    a spatial index, and level-of-detail rendering, only the visible part of the
    graph gets drawn each frame, so it stays responsive even with huge graphs.
    The downside is that hit testing has to be done manually (ArrowCrab tracks
    rectangular regions for nodes and polyline proximity for edges), but it's
    straightforward.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a Unified Grid?</h2>
  <p>
    Most graph layout libraries compute node positions and edge routes
    separately with different coordinate systems. This makes it easy for edges
    to clip through nodes, and you end up needing nudging or clipping heuristics
    to fix it. ArrowCrab puts nodes and edges on the same sparse integer grid.
    If a cell has a node in it, edges can't use it, and vice versa. So you get
    <strong>no intersections by construction</strong>, not by collision detection.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
